# Input, network, and cryptographic safeguards

Apply the relevant sections when project code introduces these capabilities.
Shared security requirements are defined in [security.mdc](security.mdc).
Coursework follows its applicable course instructions.

## Input handling

Accept the fields and structures defined by the operation's contract.
Do not automatically assign arbitrary input fields to configuration or
privileged objects.

Validate relevant types, ranges, lengths, and relationships between fields.
Distinguish missing, invalid, and unsupported values.

Bound input size, nesting, or processing complexity where external input could
cause excessive work. Avoid regex patterns with pathological execution behavior;
use simpler parsing approaches when appropriate.

For Python parsing and deserialization, follow
[secure_python_sinks.mdc](secure_python_sinks.mdc).
For SQL construction and execution, follow [secure_sql.mdc](secure_sql.mdc).

## Network destinations

Define permitted schemes and destinations for the operation.
Prefer HTTPS for external services.

Validate redirects against the same destination policy.
Account for resolved addresses when enforcing restrictions on access to local,
private, or otherwise privileged services.

Do not allow externally supplied URLs to grant unintended access to local
services, cloud metadata endpoints, or credentials.

Forward authentication material only to its intended destination.
A redirect does not authorize forwarding credentials to another host.

## Network budgets

Establish network limits appropriate to the task and service before execution.
These may include:
- Request count, rate, and concurrency.
- Per-request timeouts and an overall operation deadline.
- Response size and cumulative transfer volume.
- Service quotas, billable usage, and API or model-token budgets where applicable.

Keep enforcement within application or operator-controlled limits. Caller settings
may request smaller budgets but must not bypass those ceilings.

Count retries, redirects, pagination, and parallel requests toward the relevant
overall budget. Follow [logging_and_errors.mdc](logging_and_errors.mdc) for retry
and failure behavior.

Use available usage metadata to track consumption. Where exact cost or token use
is known only after a request, leave appropriate headroom and document that limit.
Do not describe estimates as hard enforcement.

Stop or cancel further work when a budget is exhausted, and report the outcome
explicitly. Increasing a budget follows the scope process in
[coauthoring_protocol.mdc](coauthoring_protocol.mdc).

## Cryptographic operations

Apply the inspection and reuse guidance referenced in
[coauthoring_protocol.mdc](coauthoring_protocol.mdc), including Ponytail, when
selecting existing facilities.

Use established platform or maintained library implementations for the required
operation:
- Cryptographically secure randomness for secrets and tokens.
- Appropriate constant-time comparison facilities for secret verification.
- Dedicated password-hashing facilities if password storage is introduced.
- Established authenticated-encryption facilities when encryption is required.

Distinguish ordinary checksums from authenticity verification and password
protection. A checksum alone does not establish who supplied an artifact.

Select algorithms, parameters, and key-handling mechanisms when a concrete
requirement exists. Do not introduce custom cryptographic constructions.

Follow [dependencies.mdc](dependencies.mdc) for adopting libraries or tools.

## Verification

Use [testing.mdc](testing.mdc) to select focused checks for the boundaries
introduced, including relevant invalid inputs, denied destinations, exhausted
budgets, and failure paths.

Use synthetic credentials and local test doubles where suitable. Verifying a
budget mechanism should not require consuming a substantial real-service quota.
